WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.960
Hello. My name is George Stagg.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.000
I'm an open source software
engineer working in the

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.540
tidyverse team for Posit.

00:00:10.305 --> 00:00:12.965
I'm the lead developer
of the webR project.

00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:15.025
And today,

00:00:15.025 --> 00:00:18.120
I wanted to make a video
to talk about some of the changes

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.840
in, webR 0.2.0. So, if you
don't know about webR, it's a

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.755
version of R compiled
for WebAssembly,

00:00:25.755 --> 00:00:27.695
that can run in
a web browser,

00:00:28.315 --> 00:00:30.715
and other web assembly
environments such as node.js.

00:00:31.595 --> 00:00:35.890
So this is probably my favorite
quick example of what webR can

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.130
do. This is a code block with
some R code in it, in this case,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:44.615
a linear model done in
base R. And if I hit the run

00:00:44.615 --> 00:00:48.055
code button, you can see that 
R code is run, and

00:00:48.055 --> 00:00:50.375
the results shown to
you on the screen.

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:53.630
And the important part about
this is that there's no R

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.510
server running to
execute that code.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.090
That code is being executed in
the web browser directly on this machine.

00:01:02.585 --> 00:01:05.625
This is live, and interactive.
So I can change it.

00:01:05.625 --> 00:01:06.765
I can produce a

00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:10.345
new R code and run it to get
to get real interactive live

00:01:10.345 --> 00:01:12.910
results. And then that
also includes plotting. I

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.170
can plot images there, all
directly in the web browser.

00:01:18.965 --> 00:01:20.725
So you can imagine, the

00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:25.950
applications that this opens
for uses of R on the web.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.430
And the ones I'm most
interested in are things like

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:29.550
educational content.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.015
So interactive documents and
presentations like this one,

00:01:33.015 --> 00:01:34.455
and an educational doc,

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:37.435
content, including even
things that are documentation.

00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:39.655
And then,

00:01:39.655 --> 00:01:43.000
whereby having those tactile
interactive examples directly in the

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.020
webpage. And I feel really
helps with with those use cases.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.555
Another thing we're beginning to look
at is the idea of containerization

00:01:52.255 --> 00:01:54.835
of an R application
and, in sandboxing,

00:01:55.455 --> 00:01:57.615
and taking some R
application

00:01:57.615 --> 00:02:00.450
and being able to run it in
its entirety on another machine

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.090
through the idea of
portability and distribution of

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.390
of WebAssembly apps.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.850
I am also aware of one
application, one person using

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.455
webR as a way to access
R on a tablet, in this

00:02:13.455 --> 00:02:16.115
case, they're they're running
webR in a web browser,

00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:17.760
through their tablet.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.660
On a device that otherwise
wouldn't be able to access on.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.665
So, as I say, webR, version
zero two zero is now available.

00:02:26.665 --> 00:02:30.765
The top link shown there is
a demo, webR REPL application.

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:35.920
REPL stands for read, eval,
print, loop and it's

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.160
another name for the R console
that I'm sure you're

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.120
familiar with. If you're
a JavaScript developer,

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.785
you can install webR
through NPM, or you can,

00:02:44.785 --> 00:02:46.245
run it directly from CDN.

00:02:46.785 --> 00:02:49.665
And the documentation to
integrate webR into your own

00:02:49.665 --> 00:02:52.405
programs is that
that URL there.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.330
So let me talk
about the updated,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.050
webR REPL application

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:04.125
but before I do that, I'll
just talk about the changes since version,

00:03:04.745 --> 00:03:06.365
zero one one webR.

00:03:06.905 --> 00:03:09.740
So, we updated the
source version of R from,

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.280
I think it was R four one
zero to four three zero.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.240
We've changed the
package name from

00:03:17.025 --> 00:03:20.945
r dash wasm slash web r,
this, the sort of namespace package,

00:03:20.945 --> 00:03:25.125
just to the webR package in
npm, makes it a little easier to install.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.540
And we've rebuilt the
demo application, using a more

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.140
modern framework we've we've migrated
from using jQuery to

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.280
the React framework.

00:03:36.765 --> 00:03:39.805
We've expanded the
functionality of the in-browser

00:03:39.805 --> 00:03:42.305
canvas graphics and
bitmap graphics systems.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.380
And we've made improvements
to accessibility and

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.100
internationalization in
those graphics systems.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.660
And we're supporting,
lots more R packages,

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.440
which I'll talk about
in a separate video.

00:03:53.045 --> 00:03:56.805
Something else, I'll talk
about in a separate video is,

00:03:56.805 --> 00:03:58.825
the changes to
the developer API,

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.340
And if you want to
read more about that,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.040
you can also read the
NEWS document inside the webR repo.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:11.205
So let me talk about the, webR REPL app. So it looks like this,

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:15.025
and you can see if you
saw the previous version of the REPL

00:04:15.025 --> 00:04:18.470
app, it looks a bit
different. As part of the redesign,

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:23.190
into the React
framework we've expanded

00:04:23.855 --> 00:04:26.175
the view so that
there's now this,

00:04:26.175 --> 00:04:30.255
editor in the top left corner, so
I can type R code in here and

00:04:30.255 --> 00:04:32.835
you get, code suggestions and

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.140
syntax highlighting,

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.340
all built into this
code mirror code editor.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.145
The nice thing about this is
that you can also save the

00:04:44.145 --> 00:04:47.265
files directly to the virtual
file system that webR is

00:04:47.265 --> 00:04:49.765
running in, which it takes
me to this panel here.

00:04:50.385 --> 00:04:52.780
This is the

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.660
file system viewer
built into webR,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.420
it's been expanded to have more
controls at the top to manage

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.760
files and directories on
that virtual file system.

00:05:02.555 --> 00:05:04.335
And the view itself,
the component,

00:05:04.955 --> 00:05:08.655
implementing that tree
of directories has been replaced

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.030
with a component,

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.770
a React library,

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:19.825
designed to build an
accessible tree view component.

00:05:19.825 --> 00:05:21.605
So that, users who,

00:05:22.305 --> 00:05:24.865
browse the web through
keyboard only, for example,

00:05:24.865 --> 00:05:27.660
are able to
use this component, effectively.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.320
The console is, as it was
in the previous version, the main

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.655
difference between the previous version,
and this is that it

00:05:37.655 --> 00:05:42.855
has a feature enabled that
allows users to use screen readers,

00:05:42.855 --> 00:05:45.970
to read the input and output
of that console. So if you're doing some

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.890
kind of analysis or in my case,

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.990
just generate some
random numbers,

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.190
the previous version of
this component,

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.395
that those results were
not accessible to things like

00:05:56.395 --> 00:05:58.795
screen readers, but
here they are. So again,

00:05:58.795 --> 00:06:00.555
users with accessibility
issues

00:06:00.555 --> 00:06:03.900
who need to use those tools are
able to use the R console in

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.160
this webR app effectively.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.985
And then finally, the last,
panel is the plotting,

00:06:11.925 --> 00:06:13.225
panel. So if I

00:06:14.405 --> 00:06:16.565
run a command to
produce some plots,

00:06:16.565 --> 00:06:20.085
and this is using a
built in graphics device into webR,

00:06:20.085 --> 00:06:22.650
and up calling the 
canvas graphics device.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.250
It's drawing
those plots using a

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:31.005
a web API known
as canvas graphics,

00:06:31.005 --> 00:06:34.125
and you can move
through plot history and

00:06:34.125 --> 00:06:39.190
save plots into a bitmap file or
or clear them directly from this page.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.630
The final thing I'll say
about the editor is that,

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.150
you may have noticed
earlier that as I type,

00:06:47.055 --> 00:06:50.095
code suggestions
are produced.

00:06:50.095 --> 00:06:52.815
This is using a feature built
in to the code mirror editor

00:06:52.815 --> 00:06:57.370
that, we've hooked
up to R's built-in code

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:58.430
completion engine.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.010
And that means it's,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.805
context aware of the
code you're typing. So if I type sample here,

00:07:04.805 --> 00:07:06.565
then, s i z,

00:07:06.565 --> 00:07:09.285
you can see it's suggesting
the argument size to me,

00:07:09.285 --> 00:07:12.630
and that's because the
code completion generator knows that

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:16.550
size is an argument to
the to the sample function,

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.950
which is really nice as
you're writing code to have

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.410
those suggestions given,

00:07:21.095 --> 00:07:25.195
that intelligently like that.
So that's the REPL application.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.300
I'll move on now to talking
about the HTML canvas graphics device,

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.360
which I briefly showed
in the lower portion of the screen.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.805
So the canvas graphics device
is built into the webR

00:07:41.805 --> 00:07:45.185
application and allows you to
render graphics in the web browser.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.960
We've made big improvements,
since the last release,

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:50.320
by moving from

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.475
the canvas API to
to this off screen canvas API.

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:58.435
So here's a video of producing
a plot with three hundred

00:07:58.435 --> 00:08:00.275
thousand points from the
last version of webR.

00:08:00.275 --> 00:08:01.955
You can see it's quite slow.

00:08:01.955 --> 00:08:04.580
The reason for that is
because each of those points

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:08.580
is drawn by sending a
message from the worker thread where

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.780
computation happens and in the
web assembly environment to the

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.245
main thread where that
that plot is being drawn.

00:08:15.245 --> 00:08:18.205
So dispatching and handling all
of those messages for each of

00:08:18.205 --> 00:08:19.325
those points separately,

00:08:19.325 --> 00:08:22.460
creates a big overhead and
that's what the main cause of

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.660
those performance issues were. In
the latest version of webR,

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:26.540
it's much improved.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:29.205
It's an order
of magnitude faster and it's all

00:08:29.205 --> 00:08:31.705
thanks to this this
off screen canvas API.

00:08:32.485 --> 00:08:35.810
What it does is it lets us
draw the image on the worker thread

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:38.690
and then just send a single
message with the finished image

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.550
to the main thread to be shown.

00:08:43.225 --> 00:08:46.585
Using off screen canvas
has enabled us to enable,

00:08:46.585 --> 00:08:48.665
much more advanced
text rendering features too.

00:08:48.665 --> 00:08:52.630
We can now take advantage
of text rendering provided by the

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.110
web browser that
webR is running in.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.190
So here's an example
of some code, again,

00:08:57.190 --> 00:08:58.890
running live in this slide.

00:08:59.975 --> 00:09:02.635
Showing off some of
those advanced, text features.

00:09:04.135 --> 00:09:07.980
The biggest benefit of this
is probably that webR now

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.240
supports much
better, international

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.860
scripts when, plotting.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.165
So you can see there's
some Arabic text here,

00:09:15.165 --> 00:09:18.285
some Hebrew and
some Japanese, and they're all

00:09:18.285 --> 00:09:22.225
handled, automatically now by
the webR canvas graphics device.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.970
That font
fallback that enables this,

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:29.190
also allows you to use emoji
in your plots should you want to.

00:09:30.105 --> 00:09:32.025
The nice thing about the
canvas graphics devices,

00:09:32.025 --> 00:09:35.305
because we're relying on
the web browser to plot,

00:09:35.305 --> 00:09:36.265
to draw those graphics,

00:09:36.265 --> 00:09:38.870
we can actually use font files
that are installed on the user's

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:42.150
machine. You can use system
fonts. So here, for example,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:45.030
I have, family Future as the
title, but I can change that.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.535
I can say, Helvetica if I wanted to.

00:09:49.475 --> 00:09:51.715
And this provides not only
a benefit to

00:09:51.715 --> 00:09:54.355
internationalization for all
those alternative scripts,

00:09:54.355 --> 00:09:57.730
but also accessibility because there
exist some fonts that are

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.750
designed to help users with,

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.070
reading barriers
such as dyslexia,

00:10:02.905 --> 00:10:05.385
to be able to,
comfortably read text,

00:10:05.385 --> 00:10:10.470
so there's an OpenDyslexic
is such a font.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.390
You can see that if I
rerender that image,

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:15.110
that that font is now being
used for the, the title and

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.490
the X and Y labels.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:20.555
So those users who prefer
to read text with

00:10:21.195 --> 00:10:24.655
that font are now able to do
so in webR should they wish.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.900
In addition to the
canvas graphics device,

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:31.840
webR also has updates to

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:36.700
support the standard bitmap
graphics devices 

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:37.805
built into R.

00:10:38.585 --> 00:10:41.625
So if you're running in a
browser that doesn't have,

00:10:41.625 --> 00:10:43.465
if you're running in an
environment that's not a web

00:10:43.465 --> 00:10:45.430
browser, such as
node server side,

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:48.070
or you're running a R code
that isn't aware of the canvas

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:51.750
graphics device, you can now
do so with webR. So here,

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:54.390
I have an example that this
is just loading webR into the

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:57.815
page, and then there's,
some R plotting code here,

00:10:58.435 --> 00:11:01.655
writing a png to to the
virtual file system.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:02.960
And,

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.680
once that png has been written
to the virtual file system with

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.200
webR, there are file system
APIs that lets you read that

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.675
file into a
JavaScript variable.

00:11:11.675 --> 00:11:14.075
And once you have that
data in JavaScript,

00:11:14.075 --> 00:11:15.595
you can do what you
need to do with it.

00:11:15.595 --> 00:11:18.580
You can you can offer it
to the user as a download,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.300
you can display it in the
page or if you're running server

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.400
side, you could send that
data over the network.

00:11:25.355 --> 00:11:26.715
The way this works,

00:11:26.715 --> 00:11:29.755
is that we've compiled
the cairo graphics library and

00:11:29.755 --> 00:11:31.615
pango text layout engine,

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:35.190
to WebAssembly as part of
the webR build process.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:36.850
And by compiling,

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:40.385
compiling those libraries to WebAssembly and linking them in,

00:11:40.385 --> 00:11:43.105
we can enable those advanced
text features that I already

00:11:43.105 --> 00:11:45.425
talked about for the
built in graphics devices,

00:11:45.425 --> 00:11:47.690
as well as the
canvas graphics device.

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:52.410
The main difference between
the two is that for built-in

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.770
cairo graphics with
with png, for example,

00:11:56.475 --> 00:11:59.515
those fonts, can't be read
off the user's file system.

00:11:59.515 --> 00:12:02.795
And instead, they must be
provided to cairo, through the,

00:12:02.795 --> 00:12:05.200
through the webR
virtual file system.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.280
So webR ships with a
minimal selection of Noto fonts.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.680
But if you do
have other requirements for different

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.155
scripts or different font typefaces, you
can upload those fonts to the

00:12:16.155 --> 00:12:17.295
virtual file system,

00:12:17.915 --> 00:12:20.795
and webR has been configured
to search that directory

00:12:20.795 --> 00:12:24.780
home/web_use/fonts, for
font files and use them.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:27.080
So here's an example,

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:29.615
very similar to a previous
example I showed you.

00:12:29.615 --> 00:12:32.415
The only difference is this
time it's creating a png file

00:12:32.415 --> 00:12:34.275
instead of displaying
it in a browser,

00:12:34.815 --> 00:12:37.570
and you can see the results
are very, very similar.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.930
And all those text, features
that I talked about things like,

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:44.370
ligatures in Arabic text and
the font fallback for different

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:47.245
glyphs.They're all
supported, including,

00:12:47.245 --> 00:12:49.325
the the right to left text and,

00:12:50.125 --> 00:12:51.425
various other features.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:56.590
This works, in this
example, because I've uploaded some

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.870
fonts for those alternative
scripts to the virtual file

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:01.595
system in the in
the correct place.

00:13:01.595 --> 00:13:05.375
And webR has automatically then
used them to produce this plot.

00:13:05.915 --> 00:13:06.415
So,

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.570
the last thing I want to
talk about in this video is,

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:13.810
something we're calling the
lazy virtual file system.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.390
So everything I've shown you
so far comes with a price.

00:13:16.895 --> 00:13:19.955
Increased WebAssembly
download size, data size,

00:13:20.735 --> 00:13:24.035
if you're bundling fonts,
they'll have to be downloaded

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.440
but not every user will
want all of those features.

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:30.240
So not every user will
want to use cairo graphics and download

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:31.365
those font files.

00:13:31.365 --> 00:13:33.845
Some people would just
want to do some computation and

00:13:33.845 --> 00:13:35.145
and return some numbers.

00:13:36.645 --> 00:13:39.445
The same thing applies to
files built into R already.

00:13:39.445 --> 00:13:43.190
So R ships with lots
of documentation and examples and datasets.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:47.510
And they're not necessarily
needed for every user.

00:13:48.395 --> 00:13:51.995
So it'll be nice if webR only downloaded the data it actually

00:13:51.995 --> 00:13:54.255
needs to run your R code.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:56.370
And webR 0.2.0

00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:02.210
does just that. It uses
an API provided by emscripten for their,

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:07.375
for their file system
environment. That, allows the

00:14:07.375 --> 00:14:10.595
file system to only download
the files when they're requested.

00:14:11.295 --> 00:14:13.830
So here, in this example, I'll
just open the dev tools,

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:17.350
and you'll see exactly what I
mean. So if I refresh the page,

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:20.230
you can see the R
files are downloaded

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.250
to run R and its base packages.

00:14:23.015 --> 00:14:27.255
But then if I run the command
png to use cairo graphics

00:14:27.255 --> 00:14:30.085
to build a bitmap
graphics image,

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:34.130
you can see it's only at
that point that those cairo

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:37.490
libraries are downloaded to
the device. And this,

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:39.750
and a similar thing, if
I plot something now,

00:14:40.495 --> 00:14:42.435
and I draw some text,

00:14:43.135 --> 00:14:45.795
you'll see that the font files

00:14:46.735 --> 00:14:50.390
are only downloaded at the
point of actually producing that plot.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:54.230
So if I don't use cairo graphics
and produce a plot that

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:57.785
requires those fonts, they won't
be downloaded by the user.

00:14:57.785 --> 00:15:00.505
That's really nice because
it reduces the initial download

00:15:00.505 --> 00:15:02.205
size of webR by a lot.

00:15:03.065 --> 00:15:06.185
For the application
that I've been showing,

00:15:06.185 --> 00:15:07.810
the demo REPL app,

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:10.530
in the previous version of webR
with less features that was

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.070
forty megabytes of download.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.730
In the new version, it's
only nine point five megabytes,

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.270
and that's with
the extra features.

00:15:17.785 --> 00:15:20.425
So that's only
twenty three percent

00:15:20.425 --> 00:15:21.785
of the previous download size.

00:15:21.785 --> 00:15:23.945
So that's a really
good improvement for

00:15:23.945 --> 00:15:27.970
the download size
of it of an initial webR

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.330
loading, of the engine
into a page.

00:15:32.755 --> 00:15:35.555
So that's everything I wanted
to talk about in this video.

00:15:35.555 --> 00:15:37.255
Do try out the app,

00:15:37.955 --> 00:15:41.160
and see you run
your own R script.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:42.920
And if you're a
developer, as I say,

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:45.260
you can install webR
from NPM or CDN,

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:48.840
and the documentation is
shown there on the screen of

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.635
that URL. Do go and read
the documentation if you,

00:15:51.635 --> 00:15:53.955
if you want to try webR
on your own applications,

00:15:53.955 --> 00:15:56.595
because there are some
subtleties when it comes to,

00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:00.840
when it comes to putting
those applications on the web.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:03.100
If you do create something
interesting with webR,

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:06.215
please let me know. My email
address is shown on the

00:16:06.215 --> 00:16:09.175
screen, and I'd
love to hear about it. And,

00:16:09.175 --> 00:16:11.035
thank you very
much for listening.

